# .lycheeignore — regex patterns matched against URLs lychee finds
# in scanned markdown. Each line is a Rust-flavor regex; lines starting
# with `#` are comments.
#
# NB: this file is for URL-regex exclusions only. To skip filesystem
# PATHS from being scanned, use lychee's `--exclude-path` CLI flag
# instead (the workflow does that for `agnt-bridge/README.md`). Glob
# patterns like `**/node_modules/` don't belong here — they don't parse
# as regex (the leading `**` is invalid) and they wouldn't match URLs
# anyway.

# Repo self-links. The README has ~8 of these (badge SVGs + workflow
# URLs); GitHub generates them server-side so they only break when the
# repo is renamed, at which point every other self-reference breaks
# too — link rot isn't the right tool to catch that. External github.com
# URLs (other repos, gists, etc.) are still checked.
^https?://github\.com/dotbrains/agnt(?:/|\.git|$)

# Localhost / private network refs in docs are fine — they're meant to be
# user-local (Tailscale, LAN, dev server). Verifying them in CI would be
# noise + flakes.
^https?://(127\.0\.0\.1|localhost|0\.0\.0\.0)
^wss?://(127\.0\.0\.1|localhost|0\.0\.0\.0)
^https?://[^/]*\.local(/|$)
^https?://[^/]*\.tailnet\.ts\.net

# Docker-compose / k8s service names used as example hostnames in
# self-hosting docs (e.g. `http://agnt_relay`). They never resolve in
# DNS — they're documentation-only.
^https?://agnt_relay
^https?://[a-z_]+:\d+(/|$)

# Anchor links inside the same repo (lychee can't always validate without
# a full markdown parse). Cross-file repo links are still checked.
^#

# Known transient hosts that randomly return 503/429 even when up.
^https://blacksmith\.sh/
^https://x\.com/
^https://twitter\.com/

# openai.com Cloudflare-bot-blocks GET requests with 403 even though
# the URLs resolve correctly for human browsers. Two refs at the
# moment: an `openai.com/index/codex/` reference + an `openai.com/privacy`
# reference. lychee verified 92 / 92 other links pass, so this is the
# only place we have to defer.
^https?://openai\.com/

# Example placeholders in self-hosting docs — these aren't meant to resolve.
^https?://relay\.example\.com
^https?://api\.example\.com
